One of Hagrid's unknown skills is that he's great in the kitchen. He can bake 20 rock cakes ($R$) or 30 fruitcakes ($F$) in one day. Set up Hagrid's PPF on an $x,y$ graph with rock cakes ($R$) on the vertical and fruitcakes ($F$) on the horizontal.

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
      *Plot.* A full day on rock cakes is the vertical intercept, $20$ R, and a full day on fruitcakes is the horizontal intercept, $30$ F. The PPF is the straight line between them, $R = 20 - \frac{2}{3} F$. The Q2 bundle $(20, 30)$ is marked above it.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

== Q1 | Hagrid's PPF

What is Hagrid's opportunity cost of each good?

Opportunity cost of 1R: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

Opportunity cost of 1F: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  *1R:* $\frac{3}{2}$ F. *1F:* $\frac{2}{3}$ R.

  Hagrid's day is $20$R or $30$F, so $20R = 30F$. Divide through for each good:

  #mitex(`
  1R = \tfrac{30}{20} F = \tfrac{3}{2} F \qquad\qquad 1F = \tfrac{20}{30} R = \tfrac{2}{3} R
  `)

  The two opportunity costs are reciprocals, and $\frac{2}{3}$ is the absolute slope of the PPF: rock cakes given up per extra fruitcake.
]

== Q2 | Feasibility

Hagrid wants to bake 30R AND 20F in one day. Is this inefficient, efficient, or unattainable? Use a graph or algebra to justify your answer.

Inefficient, Efficient, Unattainable: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  *Unattainable.*

  *Graph:* the point $(20, 30)$ lies outside the PPF, above and to the right of the line, so it is unattainable. At $F = 20$ the frontier only allows $R = 20 - \frac{2}{3} \cdot 20 = \frac{20}{3}$, well short of $30$.

  *Algebra (time check):* $30$ rock cakes alone take $\frac{30}{20} = \frac{3}{2}$ days and $20$ fruitcakes take $\frac{20}{30} = \frac{2}{3}$ of a day, so the bundle needs $\frac{3}{2} + \frac{2}{3} = \frac{13}{6}$ days. Under a full day is inefficient, exactly one day efficient, over one day unattainable. Either justification earns full credit.
]

#pagebreak()

== Q3 | Dynamics

It turns out Hagrid wants to slow down his baking work, and cuts his time in half. Right after, the baking industry goes through a minor revolution and improves the efficiency of everyone's baking by a factor of 2. Show both changes to his PPF.

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
    After both changes Hagrid is back to $20$R or $30$F a day.

      When Hagrid cuts back his hours, his PPF shifts in so that the intercepts are half what they were before: $10$R or $15$F.

      When the new technology arrives, his PPF shifts out to twice what it was prior to the innovation: $20$R or $30$F. This just so happens to be exactly the same as prior to his time cutback.

      Each change scales both intercepts by the same factor, so the slope never moves: his opportunity costs stay at $1R = \frac{3}{2} F$ and $1F = \frac{2}{3} R$ throughout.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

